{"authored" [{"question" "q-11717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975082/state.json" "file_sha256" "6eec2d798dc7f5a72f06bc5a6741714b2bc2facc2c86f711dc7387c957724ae9" "basis" 766 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975082/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- For the matrix $H$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(H)=5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(H)=0,$ we conclude that $H$ is not invertible. Since $H$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $H$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $J$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(J)=2\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(J)=0,$ we conclude that $J$ is not invertible. Since $J$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $J$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $K$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $H$ and $J$ is singular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975082/state.json" "file_sha256" "6eec2d798dc7f5a72f06bc5a6741714b2bc2facc2c86f711dc7387c957724ae9" "basis" 766 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975082/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975082/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813d2525e7300acba